WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.780
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Advanced.

2
00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:09.025
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.325 --> 00:00:18.665
Unit fifty-five: Global Cities and Culture. In this unit, you will discuss how culture shapes cities and their global image.

4
00:00:19.650 --> 00:00:21.046
cultural capital.

5
00:00:21.746 --> 00:00:28.807
cultural capital means a city's reputation for arts and culture; also personal cultural knowledge.

6
00:00:29.107 --> 00:00:31.943
Paris is often called a cultural capital.

7
00:00:32.643 --> 00:00:35.829
Your turn. Say: cultural capital.

8
00:00:38.529 --> 00:00:39.855
cultural capital.

9
00:00:40.850 --> 00:00:42.176
creative economy.

10
00:00:42.876 --> 00:00:50.716
creative economy means economic activity based on creativity, such as design, music and film.

11
00:00:51.016 --> 00:00:54.397
The creative economy employs many young people.

12
00:00:55.097 --> 00:00:58.388
Your turn. Say: creative economy.

13
00:01:01.088 --> 00:01:02.379
creative economy.

14
00:01:03.383 --> 00:01:04.860
cosmopolitanism.

15
00:01:05.560 --> 00:01:10.370
cosmopolitanism means openness to many cultures and nationalities.

16
00:01:10.670 --> 00:01:15.479
Singapore's cosmopolitanism attracts international talent.

17
00:01:16.179 --> 00:01:19.481
Your turn. Say: cosmopolitanism.

18
00:01:22.181 --> 00:01:23.670
cosmopolitanism.

19
00:01:24.683 --> 00:01:25.859
global city.

20
00:01:26.559 --> 00:01:33.214
global city means a city with major influence on the world economy and culture.

21
00:01:33.514 --> 00:01:36.175
Tokyo and New York are global cities.

22
00:01:36.875 --> 00:01:39.829
Your turn. Say: global city.

23
00:01:42.529 --> 00:01:43.728
global city.

24
00:01:44.717 --> 00:01:46.112
nightlife economy.

25
00:01:46.812 --> 00:01:51.807
nightlife economy means businesses that operate in the evening and at night.

26
00:01:52.107 --> 00:01:55.140
Riverside cafés boost the nightlife economy.

27
00:01:55.840 --> 00:02:00.188
Your turn. Say: nightlife economy.

28
00:02:02.888 --> 00:02:04.214
nightlife economy.

29
00:02:05.217 --> 00:02:06.473
cultural district.

30
00:02:07.173 --> 00:02:12.923
cultural district means an area of a city with many galleries, theatres and studios.

31
00:02:13.223 --> 00:02:16.488
The new cultural district hosts weekly performances.

32
00:02:17.188 --> 00:02:20.444
Your turn. Say: cultural district.

33
00:02:23.144 --> 00:02:24.447
cultural district.

34
00:02:25.450 --> 00:02:27.206
soft infrastructure.

35
00:02:27.906 --> 00:02:34.224
soft infrastructure means institutions and services such as education, culture and healthcare.

36
00:02:34.524 --> 00:02:38.254
Soft infrastructure attracts talented residents.

37
00:02:38.954 --> 00:02:42.349
Your turn. Say: soft infrastructure.

38
00:02:45.049 --> 00:02:46.816
soft infrastructure.

39
00:02:47.817 --> 00:02:49.212
cultural export.

40
00:02:49.912 --> 00:02:53.979
cultural export means cultural products sold or shared abroad.

41
00:02:54.279 --> 00:02:57.869
K-pop is South Korea's most famous cultural export.

42
00:02:58.569 --> 00:03:02.103
Your turn. Say: cultural export.

43
00:03:04.803 --> 00:03:06.164
cultural export.

44
00:03:07.150 --> 00:03:09.219
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:03:09.519 --> 00:03:13.574
Rithy, could Phnom Penh become a cultural capital of Southeast Asia?

46
00:03:14.883 --> 00:03:18.566
A recent article claimed that it already was becoming one.

47
00:03:19.850 --> 00:03:21.513
What evidence did it give?

48
00:03:22.817 --> 00:03:27.521
It pointed out that new galleries and music venues had opened along the riverside.

49
00:03:28.817 --> 00:03:32.395
Some critics warned that rising rents would push artists out.

50
00:03:33.683 --> 00:03:38.794
Yes, and one artist admitted having moved her studio twice in three years.

51
00:03:40.083 --> 00:03:45.113
So soft infrastructure, such as affordable studios, matters.

52
00:03:46.417 --> 00:03:51.948
Exactly. The writer concluded that culture could be Cambodia's next great export.

53
00:03:53.250 --> 00:03:56.329
Grammar focus: Reported speech (mastery).

54
00:03:56.629 --> 00:04:00.034
Master reported speech with precise verbs and backshift.

55
00:04:00.334 --> 00:04:05.087
The article claimed that it was becoming one. It pointed out that venues had opened.

56
00:04:05.387 --> 00:04:08.316
She admitted having moved her studio twice.

57
00:04:08.616 --> 00:04:16.002
Backshift is optional when the reported fact is still true now, but it is standard in formal narrative reporting.

58
00:04:17.017 --> 00:04:20.377
Now read and listen. Culture as a City's Engine.

59
00:04:20.677 --> 00:04:26.032
Cities increasingly compete not only for factories but also for creative talent.

60
00:04:27.317 --> 00:04:32.195
Researchers have argued that a strong creative economy attracts investment and tourism.

61
00:04:33.483 --> 00:04:39.697
Global cities invest heavily in cultural districts, museums and festivals.

62
00:04:40.983 --> 00:04:47.093
Cosmopolitanism, the openness to many cultures, is often cited as a key attraction.

63
00:04:48.383 --> 00:04:52.879
However, critics have pointed out that rising rents can drive artists away.

64
00:04:54.183 --> 00:05:00.931
Soft infrastructure, such as affordable studios and arts education, is therefore essential.

65
00:05:02.217 --> 00:05:08.628
Countries such as South Korea have shown how cultural exports can transform global image.

66
00:05:09.917 --> 00:05:14.912
Phnom Penh's growing arts scene suggests that Cambodia may follow a similar path.

67
00:05:16.217 --> 00:05:18.904
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:19.204 --> 00:05:22.388
Cultural products sold or shared abroad are blank.

69
00:05:22.688 --> 00:05:29.812
A, cultural exports. B, soft infrastructure. C, nightlife economy.

70
00:05:33.817 --> 00:05:37.163
The answer is A: cultural exports.

71
00:05:38.650 --> 00:05:41.729
She said that she blank the exhibition the day before.

72
00:05:42.029 --> 00:05:45.404
A, has seen. B, had seen. C, sees.

73
00:05:49.417 --> 00:05:52.357
The answer is B: had seen.

74
00:05:53.850 --> 00:05:56.291
He pointed out that prices blank rising.

75
00:05:56.591 --> 00:06:00.743
A, were. B, are being. C, have.

76
00:06:04.750 --> 00:06:06.866
The answer is A: were.

77
00:06:08.350 --> 00:06:10.477
Openness to many cultures is blank.

78
00:06:10.777 --> 00:06:16.161
A, cosmopolitanism. B, creative economy. C, cultural district.

79
00:06:20.150 --> 00:06:23.229
The answer is A: cosmopolitanism.

80
00:06:24.717 --> 00:06:27.831
According to the text, what can drive artists away?

81
00:06:28.131 --> 00:06:32.667
A, festivals. B, rising rents. C, museums.

82
00:06:36.650 --> 00:06:39.590
The answer is B: rising rents.

83
00:06:41.083 --> 00:06:47.033
Now it's your turn. Part one: How could Cambodia increase its cultural influence abroad?

84
00:06:52.333 --> 00:06:56.945
Part two: Report one opinion you've heard, then propose two strategies.

85
00:07:03.245 --> 00:07:13.153
A friend claimed that Khmer music could become as popular as K-pop. I'd propose funding young artists and promoting Cambodian films at international festivals.

86
00:07:13.453 --> 00:07:15.801
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:16.817 --> 00:07:21.826
Great work today! You learned eight new words and reported speech (mastery).

88
00:07:22.126 --> 00:07:26.357
Today's value is creativity. Use your imagination when you play.

89
00:07:26.657 --> 00:07:29.913
See you in Unit fifty-six. Goodbye!

90
00:07:30.213 --> 00:07:32.761
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

